## Proof

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only the question, no solutions, sketches, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

**Claim:** Yes, $f$ must belong to $L^p(I)$, and in fact $\|f\|_p \le 1$.

**Proof.**

Since each $f_k \in L^p(I)$, each $f_k$ is measurable. The pointwise limit of measurable functions is measurable, so $f$ is measurable.

By hypothesis, $f_k(x) \to f(x)$ for almost every $x \in I$. Therefore, for a.e. $x$,
$$|f_k(x)|^p \longrightarrow |f(x)|^p \quad \text{as } k \to \infty.$$

The functions $g_k := |f_k|^p$ are non-negative and measurable on $I$, and $g_k \to |f|^p$ a.e. Applying **Fatou's Lemma** to the sequence $(g_k)$:

$$\int_I |f|^p \, dm \;=\; \int_I \liminf_{k\to\infty} g_k \, dm \;\le\; \liminf_{k\to\infty} \int_I g_k \, dm \;=\; \liminf_{k\to\infty} \int_I |f_k|^p \, dm.$$

By the given bound $\|f_k\|_p \le 1$, we have
$$\int_I |f_k|^p \, dm = \|f_k\|_p^p \le 1^p = 1 \quad \text{for every } k.$$

Hence
$$\int_I |f|^p \, dm \;\le\; \liminf_{k\to\infty} 1 \;=\; 1 \;<\; \infty.$$

Since $f$ is measurable and $\int_I |f|^p\,dm < \infty$, we conclude $f \in L^p(I)$, with $\|f\|_p \le 1$. $\blacksquare$

